.\" fgets.3 - line input and string output for streams
.TH FGETS 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fgets, fputs \- input of a line from, and output of a string to, a stream
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "char *fgets(char *" s ", int " size ", FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "int fputs(const char *" s ", FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fgets ()
reads in at most one less than
.I size
characters from
.I stream
and stores them into the buffer pointed to by
.IR s .
Reading stops after an
.B EOF
or a newline.
If a newline is read, it is stored into the buffer.
A terminating null byte (\(aq\\0\(aq) is stored after the last character in the buffer.
.PP
.BR fputs ()
writes the string
.I s
to
.IR stream ,
without its terminating null byte (\(aq\\0\(aq).
.SS Removed interface: gets()
The
.BR gets ()
function is
.B not
provided by Substrate libc.
It was removed from the C standard by C11 (and from POSIX by POSIX.1-2008) because it
performs an unbounded write into a caller-supplied buffer with no way to specify
the buffer size, making any use of it a buffer-overflow vulnerability.
Programs that previously called
.BR gets ()
must be updated to use
.BR fgets ()
or
.BR getline (3)
instead (REQ-06-0254).
Note that
.BR fgets ()
preserves any trailing newline in the destination buffer, whereas
.BR gets ()
historically discarded it; callers porting old code may need to strip the
newline themselves.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fgets ()
returns
.I s
on success.
It returns
.B NULL
on end-of-file with no characters read, on read error, or when
.I size
is less than or equal to zero.
.PP
.BR fputs ()
returns
.B 0
on success and
.B EOF
on error.
.SH ERRORS
These functions do not set
.I errno
themselves; underlying I/O errors set the stream's error indicator (see
.BR ferror (3))
and may be reported by lower-level routines such as
.BR read (2)
and
.BR write (2)
via
.IR errno .
The most common conditions reported by the underlying layer are:
.TP
.B EBADF
The file descriptor associated with
.I stream
is not valid, or is not open for the required mode (reading for
.BR fgets (),
writing for
.BR fputs ()).
.TP
.B EINTR
The read or write call was interrupted by a signal before any data was transferred.
.TP
.B EIO
A low-level I/O error occurred while accessing the underlying object.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fread (3),
.BR fwrite (3),
.BR fgetc (3),
.BR fputc (3),
.BR getline (3),
.BR puts (3),
.BR ferror (3),
.BR scanf (3),
.BR printf (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fgets ()
and
.BR fputs ()
conform to ISO C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
The historical
.BR gets ()
function was removed from the C language by ISO C11 and from POSIX by POSIX.1-2008
on security grounds, and is intentionally not provided by Substrate libc.
